{
 "architecture": "## 1. Navigation map\n\nFrontend is an SPA under BrowserRouter. A global header appears on every page: brand link 'GitHub' to '/'; on the right, anonymous users see 'Sign up' (/signup), 'Sign in' (/login), and 'Forgot password' (/password_reset); signed-in users see an account menu button labelled with the current username. The account menu opens a menu containing 'Signed in as <username>', 'Your organizations' (/organizations), 'Settings' (/settings/security), and 'Sign out'. Signing out clears the session and returns to '/'.\n\n- '/' HomePage: anonymous users see public repositories (heading 'Public repositories') as links named by repository name to '/:org/:repo'; signed-in users see all repositories they can access (heading 'Your repositories'), including private repositories granted through Owner status, direct grants, or team grants.\n- '/signup', '/login', '/password_reset': AccountAccessPage is one shared component that shows the appropriate form. '/signup' fields: 'Username', 'Email', 'Password', 'Confirm password'; submit 'Sign up'. '/login' fields: 'Username or email address', 'Password'; submit 'Sign in'; it also links 'Forgot password?' to /password_reset. '/password_reset' first shows 'Email' and 'Send recovery email'; after submit it displays the fixed code '123456' and the fields 'Verification code', 'New password', 'Confirm new password', with submit 'Reset password'. Successful registration shows a status message and a 'Sign in' link; it does not create a session.\n- '/settings/security': SecuritySettingsPage, heading 'Account settings'; form labels 'Current password', 'New password', 'Confirm new password'; button 'Update password'.\n- '/organizations': OrganizationsPage, heading 'Your organizations'; lists each organization the signed-in user belongs to as a link named by display name to '/:org'; 'New organization' link goes to '/organizations/new'.\n- '/organizations/new': CreateOrganizationPage; fields 'Organization name' and 'Display name'; submit 'Create organization'; on success navigates to the new organization's '/:org'.\n- '/:org': OrgOverviewPage. Heading is the organization display name. Below it are tab links 'Repositories' -> '/:org/repositories', 'People' -> '/:org/people', 'Teams' -> '/:org/teams'. The default Repositories view lists repositories owned by the organization as links named by repository name to '/:org/:repo'.\n- '/:org/repositories': same tab shell; lists repositories visible to the current viewer (public for everyone; private only by Owner status, direct grant, or team grant).\n- '/:org/people': PeoplePage; lists members with role labels 'Member' or 'Owner'. An Organization Owner sees an 'Add member' form with label 'Username or verified email' and button 'Add member', and a 'Remove' button beside each member row.\n- '/:org/teams': TeamsPage; lists teams as links named by team name to '/:org/teams/:team'; Organization Owner sees 'New team' link to '/:org/teams/new'.\n- '/:org/teams/new': NewTeamPage; fields 'Team name' and optional 'Parent team' select; submit 'Create team'.\n- '/:org/teams/:team': TeamPage; heading is the team name; sections 'Members' and 'Child teams'. Members section has 'Add member' form (label 'Username or verified email', submit 'Add member') and 'Remove' buttons. Child teams section lists child team links and has 'Child team' select plus 'Add child team' button; each child row has 'Remove'.\n- '/:org/:repo': RepoPage; heading is the repository name; visible tab 'Settings' -> '/:org/:repo/settings'; the repo body shows 'Public' or 'Private'.\n- '/:org/:repo/settings': RepoSettingsPage; tab/link 'Manage access' -> '/:org/:repo/settings/access'.\n- '/:org/:repo/settings/access': ManageAccessPage; section 'People' has labels 'Username' and 'Role' with button 'Add people'; section 'Teams' has labels 'Team' and 'Role' with button 'Add teams'; role options are 'Read', 'Triage', 'Write', 'Maintain', 'Admin'; each grant row has a 'Remove' button.\n\n## 2. Data model\n\nAll persisted state is read and written through one store module (backend/src/store.js) backed by loadJson/saveJson in a single db file. Collections and fields:\n\n- users: id, username (unique, 1-39 lowercase ASCII), email (unique), passwordHash, verified, createdAt.\n- sessions: token, userId, createdAt.\n- organizations: id, name (unique lowercase), displayName (trimmed 1-100), createdAt.\n- orgMemberships: id, organizationId, userId, role ('owner' or 'member').\n- teams: id, organizationId, name, parentTeamId (nullable, must be a team in the same organization).\n- teamMemberships: id, teamId, userId.\n- repositories: id, organizationId, name, visibility ('public' or 'private'), createdAt.\n- repoGrants: id, repositoryId, granteeType ('user' or 'team'), granteeId, role ('read', 'triage', 'write', 'maintain', 'admin').\n\nRelationships: an organization Owner is an orgMembership with role 'owner'. Team membership is independent of organization membership, but only accounts that are organization members can be added to teams. A team grant on a repository applies only to direct members of that team; parent/child team hierarchy never carries membership. Removing an organization member atomically deletes their org membership, all their team memberships in that organization, and all their direct repo grants in that organization; team grants themselves remain.\n\n## 3. Seed plan\n\nSeed only when the data file does not exist. Passwords: every seeded account that does not have its own quoted password uses 'Valid-password-123!'.\n\nAccounts: alice-dev / alice.dev@example.test, org-owner / org-owner@example.test, existing-member / existing-member@example.test, new-member / new-member@example.test, org-member / org-member@example.test, protected-member / protected-member@example.test, repo-admin / repo-admin@example.test, team-maintainer / team-maintainer@example.test, bob-reviewer / bob-reviewer@example.test, recovery-success / recovery-success@example.test, recovery-invalid-code / recovery-invalid-code@example.test, recovery-visibility / recovery-visibility@example.test, password-change-required / password-change-required@example.test, password-change-success / password-change-success@example.test, password-change-invalid / password-change-invalid@example.test. 'unknown@example.test' and 'unknown-reviewer' are not seeded and are treated as unknown.\n\nOrganization: 'Acme Demo' with name 'acme-demo'. Owner membership: org-owner. Members: existing-member, org-member, protected-member, repo-admin, team-maintainer, bob-reviewer. new-member is an existing account but is not initially an organization member.\n\nTeams under acme-demo: 'frontend-team' (members team-maintainer, bob-reviewer), 'platform-team' (member org-member), 'frontend-child' (child of frontend-team), and 'access-role-team' (initially empty).\n\nRepositories under acme-demo: 'acme-docs' (public) and 'secret-research' (private).\n\nRepository grants initially: repo-admin has Admin on secret-research; org-member has Write on secret-research. No other repo grants are seeded, so REQ-2-3 tests start from a nearly clean Manage access page.\n\nOrganizations and teams created through REQ-2-1-2 and REQ-2-2-1 are not seeded; they are created by the scenarios in the UI.\n\n## 4. API endpoints\n\nBackend modules: backend/src/app.js wires modules; backend/src/store.js is the shared store; backend/src/seed.js seeds; backend/src/auth.js handles identity; backend/src/orgs.js handles organizations and people; backend/src/teams.js handles teams; backend/src/repos.js handles repositories and access grants.\n\nauth.js:\n- POST /api/auth/register {username, email, password, confirmPassword} -> 201 {user}; validates username format, email format, password policy, uniqueness.\n- POST /api/auth/signin {identifier, password} -> 200 {user} and sets session cookie; accepts username or email; 401 on failure.\n- POST /api/auth/signout -> 204 and clears session cookie.\n- POST /api/auth/forgot {email} -> 200 {code: '123456'}.\n- POST /api/auth/reset {email, code, password, confirmPassword} -> 200 {message}; only code '123456' and a compliant password update the same account; wrong code -> 400; clears all sessions for that account.\n- POST /api/auth/change-password {currentPassword, newPassword, confirmPassword} -> 200 {message}; requires session; invalid current password or policy violation -> 400; keeps the current session but clears the account's other sessions.\n- GET /api/auth/me -> 200 {user} or 401.\n\norgs.js:\n- GET /api/organizations -> 200 {organizations} (memberships of the signed-in user).\n- POST /api/organizations {name, displayName} -> 201 {organization}; requires session; creates the owner membership.\n- GET /api/organizations/:orgName -> 200 {organization} (public organization info plus role when signed in and member).\n- GET /api/organizations/:orgName/people -> 200 {members}; requires a signed-in organization member.\n- POST /api/organizations/:orgName/people {identifier} -> 201 {membership}; requires Organization Owner; identifier is an existing account username or verified email; default role 'member'.\n- DELETE /api/organizations/:orgName/people/:userId -> 204; requires Organization Owner; rejects if it would leave the organization with no Owner; cascades team memberships and direct repo grants.\n\nteams.js:\n- GET /api/organizations/:orgName/teams -> 200 {teams}; requires a signed-in organization member.\n- POST /api/organizations/:orgName/teams {name, parentTeamName?} -> 201 {team}; requires Organization Owner; validates unique team name within the org and no parent cycle.\n- GET /api/organizations/:orgName/teams/:teamName -> 200 {team, members, childTeams}; requires a signed-in organization member.\n- POST /api/organizations/:orgName/teams/:teamName/members {identifier} -> 201; requires Organization Owner; account must be an org member.\n- DELETE /api/organizations/:orgName/teams/:teamName/members/:userId -> 204; requires Organization Owner.\n- POST /api/organizations/:orgName/teams/:teamName/child-teams {teamName} -> 201; requires Organization Owner; same-org and no direct/indirect cycle.\n- DELETE /api/organizations/:orgName/teams/:teamName/child-teams/:childTeamName -> 204; requires Organization Owner.\n\nrepos.js:\n- GET /api/repositories -> 200 {repositories}; public for anonymous; accessible-only for signed-in users.\n- GET /api/organizations/:orgName/repositories -> 200 {repositories}; same visibility filter scoped to the organization.\n- GET /api/repositories/:orgName/:repoName -> 200 {repository}; 404/403 when not accessible.\n- GET /api/repositories/:orgName/:repoName/access -> 200 {grants}; requires Organization Owner or repo Admin grant.\n- POST /api/repositories/:orgName/:repoName/access {granteeType, granteeId, role} -> 201 {grant}; requires Organization Owner or repo Admin; grantee user must be an org member, grantee team must be in the same org; role one of read/triage/write/maintain/admin.\n- DELETE /api/repositories/:orgName/:repoName/access/:grantId -> 204; same permission requirement.\n\n## 5. Sessions and permissions\n\nSession cookie is HttpOnly, named 'session', maxAge 7 days. Sign-in creates a session; sign-out clears it; registration does not create a session. Recovery and password change invalidate other sessions for the account; recovery also invalidates the current one because recovery is entered anonymously.\n\nPublic repositories are readable by everyone. Private repositories are readable by a signed-in user only if they are an Organization Owner of the owning organization, have a direct repo grant, or are a direct member of a team with a repo grant. Roles are stored and enforced literally, not as a cumulative ladder: any of read/triage/write/maintain/admin grants private repository access; a repo Admin grant (direct or via team) additionally allows Manage access. Organization Owner status grants implicit Admin access to all repositories in the
