{
  "architecture": "The existing application is kept intact. All inherited routes, pages, API paths, seed data, and the sign-in flow continue to work. The extension adds repository creation/fork/clone/visibility, code browsing, commit history, diffs, code search, branch management, and web file editing on top of the same persisted store and the same React shell.\n\n1. NAVIGATION MAP\nApp shell (frontend/src/App.tsx): the existing top navigation bar remains on every page. Signed-out nav shows 'Sign up', 'Sign in', and 'Forgot password'. Signed-in nav gains a visible 'New repository' link next to the existing 'Account menu' button. The account menu keeps the current username heading, the user's organizations, 'Your organizations', and 'Sign out'. A header search form is added on every page: an input whose accessible name/placeholder is 'Search repositories' and a submit button named 'Search'; it navigates to '/search?q=...'.\n\nRoutes and page modules (all existing routes remain):\n- '/' HomePage: heading 'Home', section 'Public repositories' listing every public repository as a link named by repository name; it also renders the signed-out nav search. Public repository links go to the repository overview.\n- '/signup', '/signin', '/forgot-password' AccountAccessPage: unchanged.\n- '/dashboard' DashboardPage: heading 'Dashboard', sections 'Organizations' and 'Repositories'. Repositories lists every repository the signed-in user can read, named by repository name. A 'New repository' button appears here.\n- '/orgs' YourOrganizationsPage and '/orgs/new': unchanged.\n- '/orgs/:orgName' OrganizationPage: tabs 'Repositories', 'People', 'Teams' remain. The Repositories tab additionally shows a 'New repository' button for users with creation permission in that organization.\n- '/orgs/:orgName/people', '/orgs/:orgName/teams', '/orgs/:orgName/teams/:teamName', '/orgs/:orgName/teams/new': unchanged.\n- '/repositories/new' NewRepositoryPage: the form opened by 'New repository'. Label 'Owner' is a select listing the current user's personal namespace plus all organizations where the user is an Owner. Other fields: 'Repository name', 'Description' (optional), 'Visibility' with values 'Public' and 'Private', checkbox 'Initialize this repository with a README', submit button 'Create repository'. On success it navigates to the new repository overview.\n- '/search' SearchPage: heading 'Search results'. It lists accessible repositories whose name/description contains the query as links named by repository name. When no repository matches it shows visible text 'No repositories found'.\n- Repository overview route remains '/orgs/:orgName/repositories/:repoName' for organization repositories, and a parallel '/users/:username/repositories/:repoName' route is added for personal repositories. RepositoryPage heading and document.title are 'owner / repo' (for example 'acme-demo / acme-docs'). The page shows owner, repository name, 'Public' or 'Private', description, and 'Default branch: <branch>'. Tab navigation links are 'Code', 'Issues', 'Pull requests', and 'Settings'. A 'Fork' button is shown to signed-in users who can read the repository; it opens a fork dialog with 'Owner', 'Repository name', and submit 'Fork'. The 'Settings' tab goes to the existing settings route; on the settings page an Admin sees a 'Visibility' section with 'Public'/'Private' select and a 'Change visibility' button.\n- Repository Code page: '/orgs/:orgName/repositories/:repoName/code' (and equivalent for personal repos). It displays the repository and current branch/revision. At the top is a branch selector button whose accessible name is the current branch name (for example 'main'). A 'Code' button on the Code page opens a clone popover dialog (aria-label 'Clone') containing a read-only clone URL and a 'Copy' button. A 'Commits' link opens commit history. Signed-in users with Write or higher see an 'Add file' button. The main area lists files and directories at the current path; directories and files are links named by their names. Direct file URLs use '/orgs/:orgName/repositories/:repoName/blob/:ref/:path*'.\n- File page: a read-only file view showing the path and content; the heading is the file name. A user with Write or higher sees an 'Edit file' button.\n- Add file page: '/orgs/:orgName/repositories/:repoName/new/:ref'. Labels are 'File name' and 'Content'. The submit button's accessible name is dynamically 'Add <file name>' (for example 'Add pw-file-123.md').\n- Edit file page: '/orgs/:orgName/repositories/:repoName/edit/:ref/:path*'. Labels are 'File name' and 'Content'; submit button is 'Commit changes'.\n- Commit history: '/orgs/:orgName/repositories/:repoName/commits/:ref'. Heading 'Commits'. It lists commit records in reverse chronological order with message, author, and time; each commit message is a link to the commit detail page. A 'Compare revisions' link on this page opens the compare page.\n- Commit detail: '/orgs/:orgName/repositories/:repoName/commit/:commitId'. Heading is the commit id. It shows the commit message, author, time, and the changed files with a line-by-line diff.\n- Compare page: '/orgs/:orgName/repositories/:repoName/compare?base=<base>&compare=<compare>'. Heading 'Compare revisions'. Base is the earlier/revision target and compare is the newer revision; it shows changed files and a line-by-line diff.\n- Code search: '/orgs/:orgName/repositories/:repoName/search'. Heading 'Code search'. Input accessible name 'Search code' and button 'Search'. Results list matching file paths with snippets as links to the file page.\n- Branches page: '/orgs/:orgName/repositories/:repoName/branches'. Heading 'Branches'. It lists branch names, marks the default branch with 'Default', and has a 'Change default branch' button for Admins. The change-default dialog is a role='dialog' with label 'Change default branch', a select labeled 'Branch', and buttons 'Save' and 'Cancel'. Branch creation is done from the Code page branch selector dialog: input 'Find branch', branch list items, and a 'Create branch' button after typing a new name.\n\n2. DATA MODEL\nOne persisted store (db.json through loadJson/saveJson in store.js) is used by every backend module. Existing collections and fields are kept, and repositories/commits/branches extend them:\n- users: username, email, passwordHash, passwordSalt, createdAt.\n- sessions: token, username, createdAt.\n- organizations: name, displayName, createdAt.\n- memberships: orgName, username, role ('Owner'|'Member').\n- teams: orgName, name, parent.\n- teamMembers: orgName, teamName, username.\n- repositories: ownerType ('user'|'org'), ownerName (existing orgName key; for personal repositories it is the username), name, visibility ('Public'|'Private'), description, defaultBranch, sourceOwner (nullable), sourceRepo (nullable), createdAt. Unique per ownerType+ownerName+name. Existing repository records are read as ownerType='org'.\n- repoAccess: ownerType, ownerName, repoName, granteeType ('user'|'team'), granteeName, role ('Read'|'Triage'|'Write'|'Maintain'|'Admin').\n- branches: ownerType, ownerName, repoName, name, commitId. Unique per repository+branch name.\n- commits: ownerType, ownerName, repoName, id (short hash), parentId (null for root), author, message, time, files (object mapping path to full file content at that commit), changes (array of {path, status, additions, deletions}). A commit's files object is the full snapshot for that commit, so a branch points to a commit and the tree/file/compare/search views are all derived from commit.files. Editing a file copies the parent commit's files, applies the change, stores a new commit with a new id, and updates the branch's commitId; old commits are never rewritten.\nRelationships: an organization has memberships, teams, and repositories; a user has personal repositories and can be a member of organizations and teams; a repository has branches, commits, and repoAccess grants; branch points to a commit in the same repository; fork repositories store sourceOwner/sourceRepo but are otherwise independent.\n\n3. SEED PLAN\nSeeding runs only when db.json does not exist. All seeded accounts use the password 'Valid-password-123!' and verified emails. All previously seeded accounts remain (alice-dev, org-owner, existing-member, new-member, bob-reviewer, team-maintainer, repo-admin, org-member, protected-member, recovery-success, recovery-invalid-code, recovery-visibility, password-change-invalid, password-change-required, password-change-success). New accounts: repo-owner/repo-owner@example.test, fork-user/fork-user@example.test, collaborator/collaborator@example.test, visibility-admin/visibility-admin@example.test, branch-contributor/branch-contributor@example.test, default-branch-admin/default-branch-admin@example.test, default-branch-viewer/default-branch-viewer@example.test, file-contributor/file-contributor@example.test.\nOrganization: acme-demo with displayName 'Acme Demo'. Memberships: org-owner Owner, protected-member Owner, org-member Member, repo-owner Owner. repo-owner therefore has organization creation permission in addition to his personal namespace.\nRepositories and permissions:\n- acme-docs: owner org/acme-demo, Public, default main, description 'Acme demo documentation', source null. Branches: main. Commits on main authored by alice-dev: c1 'Initial commit' and c2 'Document search flow'. main files: README.md (contains 'Document search flow') and src/search.ts.\n- secret-research: owner org/acme-demo, Private, default main, source null. It is hidden from visitors and from non-authorized members in search/lists/direct links. No direct grants are seeded.\n- visibility-demo: owner org/acme-demo, Private, default main. repoAccess: visibility-admin Admin, collaborator Read.\n- branch-switch-demo: owner org/acme-demo, Public, default main. Branches: main and feature-search. main files: README.md and main-only.md; feature-search files: README.md and feature.md. repoAccess: branch-contributor Write.\n- default-branch-demo: owner org/acme-demo, Public, default main. Branches: main and release. main files: README.md; release files: README.md and release-notes.md. repoAccess: default-branch-admin Admin, default-branch-viewer Read.\n- file-management-demo: owner org/acme-demo, Public, default main. Branch main with README.md. repoAccess: file-contributor Write.\nUI-created records are not seeded because tests create them through the UI: the fork acme-docs-fork (created by fork-user in REQ-3-2-2), branches like pw-branch-<unique suffix>, and files like pw-file-<unique suffix>.md. After creation they persist on the server.\n\n4. API ENDPOINTS\nExisting host-owned endpoints (/api/health, /api/__evogit_reset, /api/__host) stay as-is. Existing app endpoints remain grouped as before, with additions shown per module.\n\nauth.js (unchanged): POST /api/auth/register, POST /api/auth/signin, POST /api/auth/signout, GET /api/auth/me, POST /api/auth/password, POST /api/auth/recovery/request, POST /api/auth/recovery/reset. All keep existing request/response shapes and session cookies.\n\norgs.js (existing endpoints kept): GET /api/orgs, GET /api/dashboard, POST /api/orgs, GET /api/orgs/:orgName, GET /api/orgs/:orgName/people, DELETE /api/orgs/:orgName/people/:username, PUT /api/orgs/:orgName/people/:username, GET/POST /api/orgs/:orgName/teams, GET /api/orgs/:orgName/teams/:teamName, POST/DELETE /api/orgs/:orgName/teams/:teamName/members, and the existing public org/repository list endpoints. They are untouched.\n\nrepos.js (repository asset management):\n- GET /api/repositories/public (optionally ?q=): public repositories; used by the home page and header search fallback.\n- GET /api/search/repositories?q=: accessible repositories for the signed-in user (public + readable private), returned as {repositories:[{ownerType,ownerName,name,visibility,description}]}.\n- POST /api/repositories body {owner, name, visibility, description, initializeRe
